{
  "hypothesis": {
    "id": "partial-fanout-crash-timing-bias",
    "parent": "crash-anchor-eligibility-base-rate-census",
    "kind": "add",
    "title": "Bias crash acceptance toward the mid-burst stratum (exactly one undelivered outbound send), holding crash volume fixed",
    "description": "In spur/spur-core/src/simulator/ at the existing crash-offer/accept site that already feeds crash_anchor.offered/applied, leave victim SELECTION untouched (the census priced re-ranking at a 1.036x ceiling) and change only the accept decision as a function of the candidate's current undelivered-outbound-send count, read from the same h1 bookkeeping the census used. Add one config field `partial_fanout_crash_bias` (f64, no-op at 0.0) gating the whole path, and — this is required, not optional — set it to a nonzero value in scheduler_configs/loop/general_vr.json so every arm actually exercises it; a merge that leaves the graded config at 0.0 measures nothing and must not be scored. Compensate the accept probability so total crashes per run stays within noise of baseline (e.g. deduct the mass added to bucket_1 from bucket_0/bucket_2+ acceptance, or renormalize against crash_anchor.steps_with_crash_eligible), because raising bucket_1 acceptance alone raises overall fault volume, and fault volume is a known independent depth driver (ordered_h3 195/230 at 6 fault events vs 0/344 at 2) that would otherwise be the entire measured effect. Report, via the already-merged crash_census/deliveryEffects block: crashes_total, crash_anchor.offered/applied, and the four inflight_bucket_{0,1,2,3plus} shares per arm, so stratum-shift, volume-shift and null are separable in one record. No new state; no edits to exec.rs/history.rs/state.rs.",
    "category": "scheduler",
    "buildsOn": [
      "crash_recovery / post_fault_ops",
      "crash_anchor",
      "delivery_effects.crash_census",
      "counters-piggyback-serialized-block"
    ],
    "expectedGain": 7,
    "expectedCost": 2,
    "rationale": "The census closed victim selection arithmetically (any_candidate_had_inflight 0.354 vs chosen-victim 0.342 => 1.036x ceiling) but explicitly left timing open and unsaturated: only ~30% of the 1.78M crash-eligible steps become crashes, so accept-side slack exists without changing crash count. The histogram is the concrete target: bucket_1 at 9.2% is rarer than bucket_2 at 16.1%, i.e. the one crash shape that leaves the victim's peer set inconsistently informed by its own last action is systematically under-sampled by uniform acceptance. This is the first anchored-crash proposal with a measured, non-saturated multiplier on its gating predicate, and it is the direction the census observation named as the remaining headroom. Scored at 4 rather than the proposer's 5 because every realized delta in this family (recovery-anchored-to-own-inflight-sends -0.0625, coverage-guided-fault-placement 0.0000, recovery-selection-null-steer-control -0.0377, delayed-send-anchored-crash, send-anchored-crash-points) has been zero or negative, so the prior on a >=1.5x rung effect is weak: the causal path to depth>=6 is real but indirect, and reweighting ~527k crashes from 9% to ~20% mid-burst is a partial, not a step-change, perturbation.",
    "generalityArgument": "The predicate is the count of a node's own undelivered outbound messages at a fault-injection accept site — no message type, role, quorum concept or protocol identifier enters the diff, and the one tunable is a dimensionless acceptance multiplier rather than a protocol-derived constant.",
    "status": "proposed",
    "branch": null,
    "prUrls": [],
    "createdAtIso": "2026-08-30T12:20:00.000Z",
    "firingCounter": null,
    "notes": "Falsification: (a) if inflight_bucket_1's share of crash decisions does not rise materially above the measured 9.2% baseline (say past ~15%) at the nonzero bias, the accept site cannot observe mid-burst states — fan-out is atomic w.r.t. the scheduler — and the partial-fanout premise is closed outright; (b) if the share rises AND crashes_total is within noise of baseline AND depth>=6/explore-second"
  },
